Ansible Architecture

Today I explored how Ansible works internally and how its components interact to automate infrastructure efficiently.

Understanding Ansible architecture makes automation clear, predictable, and scalable.

🔹 Control Node
The control node is the machine where:
Ansible is installed
Playbooks are written
Commands are executed
It acts as the brain of automation and communicates with all target systems.

🔹 Managed Nodes
Managed nodes are:
Target servers
Cloud instances
Virtual machines
They do not require any agent.
 Ansible connects using SSH or WinRM to perform tasks.

🔹 Inventory

The inventory defines:
Which hosts Ansible manages
How hosts are grouped
Environment separation

It can be:
Static (INI / YAML files)
Dynamic (AWS, Azure, GCP)

Inventory is the map of your infrastructure.

🔹 Modules

Modules are:
Small reusable units of work
Used to perform tasks like install, copy, start services

Examples:
yum / apt
service
copy
file

Modules are what actually do the work on managed nodes.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Control node runs automation.
 Inventory defines targets.
 Modules execute actions.
